---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../config";
---

<Base title="Services" description="AI-assisted development, AI-powered features and agents, architecture and modernization, regulated systems, and fractional technology leadership.">
  <h1>Services</h1>
  <p class="lede">Five kinds of work, all with the same habits: write it down, test it, review it.</p>

  <section class="service" id="ai-assisted-development">
    <h2>AI-assisted development, done carefully</h2>
    <p>
      AI coding tools can make a team dramatically faster, or quietly fill a codebase with
      plausible-looking mistakes. Which one you get depends on process. I use Claude Code
      and Cursor every day inside a spec-first workflow: written specs and decisions up
      front, tests that trace back to requirements, and human review of every change. I help
      teams adopt the tools and that discipline together.
    </p>
    <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit if:</strong> your developers are experimenting with AI tools and you want consistency, or you've tried them and the results were uneven.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service" id="ai-features">
    <h2>AI-powered features &amp; agents</h2>
    <p>
      Adding an LLM to a product is easy to demo and hard to make dependable. I design and
      build AI features, multi-step agents, and Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers that
      connect AI tools to your systems. I build them with clear boundaries on what the AI can
      touch, tests that check behavior, and an honest look at where a model shouldn't be in
      the loop at all.
    </p>
    <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit if:</strong> you have a use case in mind and want it built properly, or you need to know whether AI is the right tool for it.</p>
    <p><strong>See it in practice:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/vpciii/opn-mcp">opn-mcp</a> is a read-only MCP server that
        lets AI assistants monitor a firewall's security health without being able to change
        anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/vpciii/agent-framework">agent-framework</a> is a
        work-in-progress orchestration layer where AI agents plan, build, and check each
        other's work, with a human signing off on every change.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="service" id="architecture">
    <h2>Architecture &amp; modernization</h2>
    <p>
      Plenty of important software is ten or twenty years old and still running. I've spent a
      career bridging old systems and new ones, from lab instruments to Java/Spring
      applications. I modernize in steps you can reverse, keeping the current system running
      until its replacement has proven itself. AI tools now make that kind of migration faster
      to plan and safer to test. For new systems, I help you make the architectural decisions
      that are expensive to reverse, and write them down.
    </p>
    <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit if:</strong> you have a critical application only one person understands, or you're about to make a big technical bet.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service" id="regulated-systems">
    <h2>High-stakes &amp; regulated systems</h2>
    <p>
      Nearly thirty years of healthcare software taught me how to build systems that handle
      sensitive data: HIPAA-compliant architecture, security reviews and remediation, and
      HL7 / Mirth Connect integration with hospital EMRs. The same habits carry over to any
      domain where privacy, auditability, or safety matter.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      I'm an engineer, not an auditor or lawyer. I make systems defensible; formal compliance
      attestation is a separate service.
    </p>
    <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit if:</strong> your system touches health, financial, or personal data, or it has to integrate with healthcare systems.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service" id="leadership">
    <h2>Fractional technology leadership</h2>
    <p>
      Small organizations and nonprofits often need senior technical judgment but can't
      justify a full-time CTO. I've served as a nonprofit's CIO for over a decade, handling its
      technology strategy, vendor and build-vs-buy decisions, infrastructure, and budget. I
      also mentor University of Pittsburgh capstone teams.
    </p>
    <p class="fit"><strong>Good fit if:</strong> you're making technology decisions, including whether and how to use AI, without a senior technologist in the room.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button" href={site.bookingUrl} data-booking>Book a call</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="/contact/">Send a message</a>
  </div>
</Base>
